  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Description and Properties
    
    Sholong's Codex was a genealogical tome bound in eternal ice, its pages crafted from aurora-glass thin enough to transmit light through the inscriptions — celestial alignments and bloodline records that established factional legitimacy through constellation-mapping. Created during the Great Thaw, it was, by every formal assessment conducted before its destruction, the finest intact aurora-glass work on the Berg: luminance active, ice-binding showing no stress, aurora-glass unfractured. Frost-ward Xumaijang, an archivist compiling artifact registries for the Accord judiciary, noted its pristine condition with unease. Pristine artifacts make archivists nervous for good reason. Things that have survived everything are usually about to survive one thing less.
    
    A Berg archivist confirmed across three separate inspections that the Codex's inscribed text rearranged itself depending on the viewer's angle of approach — a property no one could explain and no one successfully replicated in other aurora-glass works. The implication, which every faction drew independently, was that the aurora itself was adjudicating competing claims. That every faction read its own legitimacy in the rearrangement should perhaps have tempered this confidence.
    
    ## Factional Role
    
    The Codex served as the ideological anchor for Holdborn (ice-thresh), the splinter faction that broke from the E'frostlings during the Great Thaw under Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam's banner. Yeaiu qi'Gleam carried the Codex when she led the schism at Terrace Momiou. Whether Holdborn seized or liberated the artifact depends entirely on the source; the Codex's provenance was formally documented but contested between factions whose claims no assessor would adjudicate — a diplomatic posture indistinguishable from cowardice.
    
    Holdborn subsequently cited the Codex to authenticate their seizure of The Beacon Stack, a territorial claim still invoked in formal accords governing that site's resource harvest. Authentication stamps bearing the Codex's imprimatur once guaranteed passage through Holdborn trade routes, extending the artifact's authority into a form of transit credential. The stamps' currency has declined alongside Holdborn's territorial losses. The Codex's formal status within Aurora Stack's factional registry is now under review — a procedural action that may extinguish even this residual authority.
    
    ## Destruction and Aftermath
    
    During the early Faction Wars, a Nightshelf raiding party shattered the Codex against the volcanic vents near the Lower Meridian. Whether this was deliberate or accidental cannot be established; no witnesses survived to clarify. Some who studied the fragments proposed the Codex broke under the weight of its own incompatible truths — aurora-inscribed text that proved every faction simultaneously right and doomed. This is a poetic reading. The volcanic vents are hot and aurora-glass is fragile. Not every destruction requires a metaphysical explanation.
    
    The fragments scattered across both colonies' territory. Some melted into stone. Some were gathered by collectors. Some were deliberately preserved broken by parties who needed the *shattered* Codex more than the whole one — a motivation I find entirely credible and insufficiently examined in the existing literature. Holdborn claims the largest shard still proves their legitimacy. The E'frostlings counter that only destruction could have ended the argument.
    
    Both positions are self-serving. Neither is wrong.
    
    An aurora-trained observer, Xuezhaihei Kria'Grace, reported a prophetic vision of the Codex appearing whole, its pages functioning as seals corresponding to renegotiations of the Flipper Accord. I note without comment that the Flipper Accord has been renegotiated more times than the public record reflects.
